A pilot program can establish that a measure works without
establishing that the system required to deliver it can reproduce that success
at scale. 

That distinction matters in utility energy-efficiency, where
pilots are treated as the bridge between bright idea and standard program
offering. We test the idea or measure, see customer participate, support
contractors installing it, measure savings, and conclude that we have learned
what we need to know.

But what have we proved?

We have proved that the intervention can work under a
defined set of conditions. That is important. It is not the same as proving
that a program can repeatedly deliver those results across a much larger and
more diverse population. 

## What Does a Pilot Actually Prove?

A successful pilot program might tell us less than we think.

At its most basic, a pilot demonstrates that an
intervention worked under a defined set of conditions. That is valuable
evidence, but does not necessarily tell us whether the program can reproduce
those results across a larger and more diverse population.

Even technical performance should be understood as an
evolving hypothesis. Early savings estimates can become more precise as our
program generates real-world data, revealing differences between predicted and
realized performance. So while technical viability is foundational to scale, it
is not the same as scalability. 

The same problem appears on the demand side. A pilot may
attract customers through intensive outreach, strong incentives, or pent-up
demand. That tells us who responded under those conditions; it does not
necessarily create durable demand across the broader market. As motivated
customers are reached, acquisition can become substantially more difficult.

Contractor capacity presents another potential blind spot. A
pilot may succeed with a small group of highly trained vendors who are willing to
absorb training, administrative requirements, or program uncertainty. Their
performance may not reflect what the broader contractor market can sustain. 

These are not failures of pilots. They are limits on what
the technical evidence can support.

The question is what happens when the conditions that made
that pilot successful begin to change.

## Scale Changes the Problem

At pilot scale, weak systems can be masked by human effort.

For pilot programs, experienced staff can easily solve
problems, provide intensive customer support, rely on especially engaged
contractors, coordinate informally, and make exceptions with the process breaks
down. 

Those workarounds are useful for learning. But they are also
a warning.

A process that works for 50 projects because senior staff
members can push it through may fail at 500 or 5,000 projects. A contractor network
that performs well with a handful of highly capable organizations may look
different when a program needs dozens of firms who are installing the measure
for the first time. A customer acquisition strategy that works with motivated
early adopters may weaken when the program has to reach households with less
awareness, few resources, or less incentive to participate. 

The question for a scalable pilot is not only: Did the
measure work? 

It is: What did the pilot demonstrate to give us confidence to
move this from pilot to full program?

## What Should a Scalable Pilot Actually Test?

My research points to six conditions that determine whether
a pilot is positioned to scale. 

This evidence suggests that the barriers to
energy-efficiency deployment extend well beyond technical performance. They
include customer demand and access, financial and cost structures, contractor
and workforce capacity, organizational processes, data and operational systems,
and the institutional conditions that shape how programs are funded, governed,
and adapted. 

The result is a recurring gap: we often have evidence that a
measure can work, but much less evidence that the delivery system surrounding
it can reproduce those results at a large scale.

A scalable pilot should therefore test more than itself.

1\. Measure viability: Does it actually work?

Technical performance is the foundation.

A measure must produce real, measurable, and cost-effective
savings. But early savings estimates should be treated as hypotheses that
become more precise through implementation experience, rather than fixed values
that can simply be carried forward as volume increases.

The pilot should establish whether performance holds under
real delivery conditions and what may need to change as implementation
improves.

2\. Market viability: Will enough customers participate?

A pilot can make demand look stronger than it really is.

Ideally, early participants may be more motivated, more
informed, or easier to reach than the broader market. Intensive outreach and
strong incentives can also create conditions that are difficult to reproduce
indefinitely.

The important question is whether participation reflects
demand beyond the initial cohort. A scalable pilot should help identify who
responds, why they respond, and what barriers emerge as the program moves
beyond early adopters.

3\. Customer reach: Can the program consistently find those
customers?

Having a market is not the same as having a way to reach it.

Programs need to understand how customers are acquired, what
it costs to reach them, which channels are effective, and whether those
channels remain viable as participation grows. Customer education, trust,
incentives, and the experience of participating all affect that equation.

The goal is not simply to demonstrate that customers will
enroll. It is to develop a repeatable customer-access mechanism that can
operate without extraordinary levels of staff intervention.

4\. Delivery capacity: Can the market deliver the work
repeatedly?

Programs ultimately depend on contractors, vendors, trades,
and other workforce resources that are often serving multiple markets at once.

A pilot may succeed because a small group of highly capable organizations
are willing to take on the risk that comes with participating in a new program.
That does not necessarily demonstrate that the broader contractor market can
make that same investment.

A scalable pilot should begin testing the network it will
eventually depend on: how many firms can participate, how quickly they become
productive, what barriers limit participation, and how much work the available
workforce can realistically deliver over a certain amount of time.

Training, technology, certification, and administrative
requirements are part of this infrastructure to consider up front, not issues
to solve after scaling begins.

5\. Operating capacity: Can the organization manage
increasing volume?

At small scale, teams can compensate for inefficient
processes through manual work, individual expertise, and informal coordination.

At larger scale, those same workarounds can become
bottlenecks.

A pilot should therefore test the operational system behind
the intervention: applications, approvals, data collection, quality assurance,
reporting, coordination, and escalation. The goal is to identify which processes
can support greater volume and which need to be standardized, automated,
consolidated, or shifted to another partner.

6\. Organizational and contextual readiness: Can the model be
sustained and adapted?

Scale is rarely a simple matter of copying a successful
pilot into a larger geography.

Programs operate within different funding structures,
organizational arrangements, regulatory environments, markets, and workforce
conditions. What works in one context may require adaptation in another.

The pilot should therefore clarify what is transferable,
what is context-dependent, and what institutional capacity is required to
support expansion, including: governance, funding, program management, market
knowledge, and workforce development.

Taken together, these six conditions shift the question from
“Did the pilot work?” to “What did the pilot demonstrate about our ability to
deliver this result repeatedly?”

I’ve developed these operating conditions into a more
detailed [pilot scalability scorecard]({{ site.baseurl }}/files/pilot_scalability_scorecard.pdf),
with evaluation questions and indicators that can be used to assess a pilot’s
readiness for broader deployment. 

## This Does Not Mean Every Pilot Must Solve Everything

Technical performance matters.

Some programs will scale more easily than others.
Infrastructure can be built after the pilot. Replication always requires some
amount of adaptation. And a small pilot cannot realistically solve every
workforce, technology, funding, or institutional challenge that may appear. 

The point is different: When scale is the intended
destination, the pilot should deliberately test the assumptions that will
determine whether scale is possible.

A 50-home pilot does not need to build a statewide workforce
pipeline. 

But if it achieves success only because it uses a highly
successful contractor, bypasses typical operations channels, or relies on
manual processes that cannot survive at higher volume, it may be testing a
delivery model that was never realistic to scale.

That Distinction changed how I interpret what I have seen in
program implementation. I see a pilot success as having two dimensions. 

First, does the intervention work?

Second, does the pilot help build the evidence, processes,
relationships, and infrastructure needed to reproduce that success?

## What should program managers do differently?

That leads to three operational shifts.

**Shift evaluation from
the measure to the delivery system.**

Do not stop at metrics such as kilowatt-hours saved per
dollar spent. Track the operational conditions behind those results: customer
acquisition costs, processing time, production turnaround, contractor
retention, administrative friction, and other indicators that reveal whether
the system can carry more volume.

**Pilot the program,
not just the measure.**

Test customer acquisition strategies, contractor engagement,
incentive structures, application processes, data requirements, quality
assurance, and administrative workflows alongside the intervention itself. The
goal is to discover operational constraints while they are still manageable.

**Design pilots around
scalability tests.**

Identify the assumptions that must hold at larger volume and
establish measurable scale. Establish measurable scale gates for contractor
capacity, customer acquisition, processing time, workforce availability, and
administrative burden. 

A pilot should generate evidence about what the program can
realistically support, not simply produce justification to expand. 

For me, the most useful management question is now also the
simplest: before asking whether a pilot worked, ask what the pilot actually
demonstrated and which conditions remain untested.

A technically successful pilot may be the beginning of a
scalable program.

It is not, by itself, evidence that one exists.

